so last week or the week before we  covered Donald Trump calling on a  Kurdish journalist by saying mr. Kurd  and of course the Kurds being an ethnic  group saying mr. Kurd ask your question  would be like saying mr. Jew or mr.  black and it didn't really make any  sense but what is a great thing that  came from that is that it reminded us of  my interview with Steven Mansfield about  the Kurds and Kurdistan and how the  Kurds fit into the future of the Middle  East which we did back in November of  2014 and what's amazing about this  interview is that it would seem like one  of those interviews that appeals to a  slice of our audience really  significantly and a lot of people just  don't care about it but this interview  ended up doing extraordinarily well  hundreds of thousands of views on  YouTube if not more and for not more  than half a million at this point and it  is a really fascinating interview and  Steven Mansfield is excellent on this  topic of the Kurds so that's where we  are starting this week in terms of  classic interview with my interview from  November of 2014  Steven Mansfield about the Kurds and  Kurdistan I'm joined today by Steven  Mansfield who's the author of the  miracle of the Kurds a remarkable story  of hope reborn in northern Iraq and I  think a great place to start Steven is  your book focuses on northern Iraq but  it would be important I think for the  audience to understand the Kurds as a  group beyond just Iraq so let's start  there who are the Kurds well it's  probably most important to know that the  Kurds are non Arab people who consider  themselves the descendants of the  ancient Medes people probably know this  term best from their Bibles here the  laws of the Medes and the Persians that  we read about in the Old Testament and  they are a unique people in the Middle  East having lived largely in the  mountains those who just like them  called them the hillbillies of the  Middle East but they're they're far more  progressive than that they have strong a  strong sense of tradition a strong sense  of belonging a strong sense of culture  and their graciousness their hospitality  combined with their warrior code they're  sort of poet warriors some have said  makes them distinct in the Middle East  they I think it's also important for  people to know that they  have been mistreated time and again  though they though they are certainly  large enough to warrant their own  homeland their region up in the north  around the Zagros Mountains over lay  parts of Turkey parts of Iran parts of  Syria and of course Iraq has has not  been you know they the boundaries have  not been drawn in all the treaties of  Europe after World War one in more  recent times so they could have their  own homelands so they've ended up being  a minority people amongst many of the  nations in the Middle East and that of  course is their historic complaint  what's brought them to international  attention in recent decades of course is  Saddam's atrocities against them so we  have this group that is largely it's  correct to say it's it's mostly a Sunni  Muslim group correct that's correct we  have this group that has there they're  predominantly living in specific areas  of a variety of countries as you've  mentioned one of the most interesting  things to me about the Kurds is if we go  back to the Ottoman Empire right we have  these circumstances where in a lot of  the recent conversations on corporate  media including the kind of kerfuffle on  the Bill Maher program one of the things  that I think was missing from the  analyses that was being made by  everybody was that before the Ottoman  Empire pre dissolution of the Ottoman  Empire the Muslim people were much more  united as a people so to speak and that  now you see very significant differences  between Muslims in different parts of  that part of the world something similar  has happened with the Kurds has it not  where they are no longer even though  they all I guess we could say share this  general desire for for a land that is  their own they are not really a unified  group in any sense of the word  well the Kurds do have their divisions  they've had an embarrassing civil war  right at a time they might have been  taking their control of their own  destiny in their hands back down just  after the no-fly zone in the early 90s  and of course what really has caused  them grief in recent years is that so  much of what they have suffered even  during the Saddam years was a result of  mistreatment by their Islamic brothers  i-i sat with the senior mullah of  Kurdistan not too long ago and he told  me that he had no intention of letting  the more extremist kind of Islam that  had done them damage for so many years  into his region the Kurds are very  moderate they have women on their  Supreme Court that women commanding  Peshmerga troops of female entrepreneurs  there's a Christian department in the  KRG the Kurdish regional government so  they actually have a government devoted  to Christians devoted to his ease  they're very moderate are very open to  other religious expressions but you're  absolutely right they're suspicious of  the surrounding Islamic world because  that world has conspired against them so  much in recent decades can you talk to  us a little bit Stephen about you know I  think one of the most important things  right now  because of all of the misinformation  that is that that is often spread about  the so called inherent violence of any  any population that is Muslim I want to  talk a little bit about the Kurds in  that context so we can not kind of  caricature them or lump them in with any  group they don't deserve to be in my  research I came across what I would call  numerous individual or anecdotal  instances stories of kidnappings by  Kurds violence against Christian  minorities by Kurds etc contextualize it  for us in terms of what can we look at  these incidents as isolated in some way  and if so why or what does it really  tell us about the Kurds well anyone  who's making the case that the Kurds are  an angelic people are making a foolish  case this is this is a people group of  as many as 30 million people will there  be crimes will there be religious  bigotry  will there be violence of course there  will on the whole though what's  appealing about the Kurds is their  moderation are the few very very few  killings that took place once they had  their affairs in their own hands and  they were protected from Saddam I would  never try to make the case that there  are people you know free of violence  free of religious bigotry in fact one of  the things I put in my book that  concerns me is that they share with the  rest of the Muslim world in the Middle  East portion of the most of the world  anyway a rise in honor killings  particularly in the rural  you have you know women who will go to  the cities go to universities learn sort  of Western ways or looser ways than  perhaps their rural context would want  religiously and there are honor killings  so so to speak of course I would  consider them dishonor killings but you  understand that's the term that's used  yes and so this this concerns me greatly  so it's wise that we've set this in  context no one is saying that the Kurds  are without some of the violence some of  the persecution I've had Christian  friends assassinated in in Kurdish  cities the difference perhaps is that  the Kurdish government came to those of  us and apologized and said this is rare  statistically it was rare so you do have  a more moderate slightly more peaceful  people than you do in the surrounding  area but again no one's claiming that  they're angels and there's they're  subject to the same religious extremism  as other peoples with regard to the  current battle against Isis and the the  possibilities of any kind of alliance  between the u.s. and the Kurds how do  you see the Kurds fitting into this  current incursion that involved Isis  well the Kurds are the most effective  fighting force on the ground against  Isis what's amazing about them in my  view is that they still see themselves  as a US ally despite the fact that the  u.s. government has betrayed them time  and again in fact one of the worst  gassings in human history happened in  1988 in a city called Halabja and some  of the gas that fell on Halabja was sold  by US companies to saddam time and again  there have been betrayals and yet the  the Kurds see themselves as US allies so  they're willing to fight Isis they want  to fight Isis they understand the threat  of Isis what they need is arms from the  West particularly the US and close air  support but there are very very  effective fighters they've proven this  time and again they have a martial hist  a very strong martial tradition that  goes back pre ottoman times and it  certainly was enhanced during the  Ottoman times they teach their young  from the very beginning to fight you  know to shoot on one foot and then shoot  on the other to develop balance and and  good targeting and what's interesting  about that to me Stephan not to  interrupt is that in many other contexts  hearing about a group that teaches their  children how to fight would be seen as  an incredibly  negative and concerning thing right in  other words in the absence of a  strategic specific that that would make  that an advantage we would say we should  be horrified that there is a group that  is teaching its children how to shoot  guns standing on one foot and this is  what concerns me because time and time  again we've seen the u.s. armed groups  that then we eventually end up fighting  against our own weapons right and and so  what can you tell us about how this  situation if you believe it would be  different why would it be different than  those countless situations the u.s. gets  itself into well I certainly understand  your concern and I I wouldn't lay it  aside at all I think it's valid the  difference perhaps with the Kurds as  they proven themselves time and again  they are not in mating of the nations  they have never missed used what  material was given to them they have  they have no designs no I'm know  imperial designs they they have I guess  the best way to say it is they've proven  themselves again and again whenever you  arm a people you take a risk there's no  question that you you know you may see  those weapons in another context we've  certainly seen that in Afghanistan we've  seen it elsewhere  but if there are any people on the earth  in my view have proven themselves time  and again capable of restraint capable  of using the weapons for the purposes  for which they were given it's the Kurds  and that we're talking about decades now  that they have been a people though they  maintain a martial culture it's not too  unlike you know a father in Alabama  teaching his sons to hunt I mean it's  about it's approximately at that level  we're not talking about militias here  for sixteen year olds or fourteen year  olds but nevertheless yes it's it's  something we would need to watch but I'm  not sure we have any other options right  now we don't want to put us boots on the  ground against Isis Isis is only about  thirty thousand more years of the Kurds  or the Peshmerga are eighty to a hundred  thousand  they just need arms they need strategy  they need close support and I think that  we can defeat Isis so we'll have to you  know we'll have to take that risk I  think as I'm not sure we have any other  options and they're the best investment  that I can see in the region all right  very very interesting and the book is  fascinating to remind everybody we've  been speaking with Steven Mansfield and  the book  the miracle of the Kurds a remarkable  story of hope reborn in northern Iraq  Steven really a pleasure having you on  thank you great to be with you David

